---
functor: 1
title: Labels and references
---

set heading(numbering: none)

A label names a heading, an equation or a block. A reference to the label shows its number.

# Labels

| Target | Label |
|:--|:--|
| Heading | `# Fields {#sec:fields}` |
| Display equation | `$$`, the math, then `$$ {#eq:work}` |
| Block | `::: theorem {#thm:main}` |

A label is a prefix, a colon and a name. The prefix is a convention: `sec`, `eq`, `thm`, `lem`, `def`, `fig`. Each label must be unique.

# References

```fn
# Fields {#sec:fields}

$$
W = F cdot d
$$ {#eq:work}

::: theorem {#thm:main}
Text.
:::

See @sec:fields, @eq:work and @thm:main.
```

| Reference | Shows |
|:--|:--|
| `@sec:fields` | Section 1 |
| `@eq:work` | (1) |
| `@thm:main` | Theorem 2 |

A reference to a label that does not exist is an error, with a "Did you mean" hint. To write a plain at sign, write `\@`.

# Citations

`[@knuth84]` shows the key in square brackets. `[@a; @b]` gives two keys. Bibliography files come later.
